{"name":"China Clay","hex":"#718b9a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"china clay","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of kaolin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/china_clay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kaolinite","blocks":[{"text":"Kaolinite ( KAY-ə-lə-nyte, -⁠lih-; also called kaolin) is a clay mineral, having the chemical composition Al2Si2O5(OH)4. It is a layered silicate mineral, with one \"tetrahedral\" sheet of silicate tetrahedra (SiO4) linked to one \"octahedral\" sheet of aluminate octahedrons (AlO2(OH)4) through oxygen atoms on one side, and another such sheet linked through hydrogen bonds on the other side."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Kaolinite_from_Twiggs_County_in_Georgia_in_USA.jpg/330px-Kaolinite_from_Twiggs_County_in_Georgia_in_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaolinite"},{"title":"China","blocks":[{"text":"China, officially the People's Republic of China (PRC), is a country in East Asia. It is divided into 33 province-level divisions, including two special administrative regions. Beijing is the capital, while Shanghai is the most populous city by urban area. Its geography features the Central Plain, major rivers such as the Yangtze and Yellow River, deserts, subtropical and temperate forests, plateaus, and mountain ranges such as the Himalayas. It is the world's second-most populous country after India, with a population exceeding 1.4 billion. Covering 9.6 million square kilometers (3,700,000 sq mi), it is the third largest country by area."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China"},{"title":"Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Clay is a type of fine-grained natural soil material containing clay minerals. Most pure clay minerals are white or light-coloured, but natural clays show a variety of colours from impurities, such as a reddish or brownish colour from small amounts of iron oxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg/330px-Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Beevers_crystal_structure_model_of_Kaolinite.jpg/500px-Beevers_crystal_structure_model_of_Kaolinite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Beevers_crystal_structure_model_of_Kaolinite.jpg/500px-Beevers_crystal_structure_model_of_Kaolinite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kaolinite structure, showing the interlayer hydrogen bonds","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeevers_crystal_structure_model_of_Kaolinite.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mica_T.png/120px-Mica_T.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mica_T.png/250px-Mica_T.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Mica_T.png/120px-Mica_T.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMica_T.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Mica_dO.png/120px-Mica_dO.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thum